{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_50","type":"agent_message","text":"RESULT: FINDINGS\n\n**1. Feed chrome**\n\n1. Keep One’s readable cards. [Pass 10](/Users/farzanm4/Desktop/repos/oparax/scratch/design-recovery/focus-review/renders/feed-open.png) already has a title; “doesn’t have a header” calls for useful context around it.\n2. The bottom-left bubble opens one floating panel: navigation, then Deck’s source groups, then account controls. Groups expand, show three sources, then an underlined “Show more” separated from the next heading. Rows contain logo, name and aligned count. Remove the name/handle switch.\n3. Honor “When it’s closed, it’s closed”: no remaining rail, no page movement. Escape closes the panel and returns focus to the bubble.\n4. Header: Feed and actual checking status left; Clustered/Direct beside them; one compact lifted summary right containing “This week” with its chart and “Free week” with its meter. This restores the objects he misses without reinstating three large tiles.\n5. Since filters were tentative, put kind and source selection in the floating panel; show only the active selection as a removable pill above stories, in both views. An empty selection must show its empty result, not silently substitute everything.\n6. “The banner itself looks good”: retain its position and dismissal, but make it a thin notification ribbon with “Message Oparax on X,” without a card shadow. Likely council difference: a separate Sources button; I favor the existing bubble.\n\n**2. Onboarding**\n\n1. “The left side is for my timeline”: retain all eight engine steps, including skipped search and failures, in their existing order. Remove duplicate step summaries from the center.\n2. “The right side is for the user’s own stuff”: profile first, then posts arriving vertically under “Reading your newest posts.” Omit the optional pinned post.\n3. Center: candidate logos and names appear as gathering returns them; during checking, an amber activity mark runs and completed candidates settle into Strong match, Possible match and Set aside, as in [Deck building](/Users/farzanm4/Desktop/repos/oparax/scratch/design-recovery/focus-review/renders/deck-building.png). Use short movement, reduced-motion support and quiet status announcements.\n4. At Choose sources, center becomes chosen accounts, RSS feeds and websites, grouped separately. Move “Your brief” to the right-column top. Show his entered sentence there first: the engine generates the fuller brief at step seven, so stream it only then. This timing is a likely council discussion point.\n5. Top right: editable “X account” and “Build my agent” before starting; lock repeat submission during the run. Heading progresses through “Choosing sources,” “Saving your agent,” and “Your agent is ready”; completion offers “Open your feed.”\n6. Setup keeps required handle, sentence and Continue, carrying both forward. “That doesn’t mean they have to connect it”: accepting a public handle must not require X authorization. Profile lookup, post reading, candidates and results belong on onboarding.\n\n**3. Login**\n\n1. Choose **Deck’s login composition as shown**: “I like the login for the Deck more.” [Deck](/Users/farzanm4/Desktop/repos/oparax/scratch/design-recovery/focus-review/renders/deck-login.png) gives the story fan substantial, readable presence; One scales it to 60% and darkens it.\n2. Restore the full-size fan beside the form on the same lit stage. Keep the front story readable and the form unobstructed. Preserve email/password, sign-up swapping in place, and neutral X/Google buttons.\n3. This already uses DESIGN.md’s theme and depth. I inspected the React Bits Pro guidance; `reactbits-auth.md` is absent and the catalog does not describe individual auth layouts. Others may favor a new block, but choosing an uninspected slug would be guessing.\n\n**4. Sources**\n\n1. “There should be no numbers next to the sources”: use one lifted list surface with section headings and row separators, replacing the current four-card arrangement. Remove group totals and article counts.\n2. Row anatomy: logo, name beside handle or website address, then a distinct Remove action. The chosen-source reason appears directly underneath, readable immediately.\n3. The inconsistent expansion comes from checking `focus`; render the product’s selection reason consistently. Missing preview reasons must be identified as missing, never invented.\n4. “Add or remove sources as per their limit count”: put Add source beside one capacity summary showing used/allowed for the applicable configured allowance. At capacity, explain what must be removed or upgraded; never invent a new limit.\n5. Build a small One Settings page now for account and subscription, reached from the bubble. Show existing account, plan and management controls. Additional billing features can wait.\n\n**5. Notifications**\n\n1. “There won’t be a toggle”: replace it with a button opening X’s message composer for the bot, with brief instructions to send the message.\n2. Opening X does not establish connection. Show waiting until the product confirms receipt, then connected status.\n3. “Notifications are fine”: retain the current compact section and adopt the shared header. Add no speculative channels.\n\n**6. Live feed**\n\n1. For “actual examples” for Farzan and Reshad, choose the **real product locally on a separate port**, with each person’s own account and agent.\n2. The current replay only highlights an existing story. A longer replay can demonstrate motion but cannot satisfy this request; waiting for the full production conversion delays useful feedback.\n3. Real arrivals should update the feed without displacing what he is reading. Show a new-story notice when he has scrolled down.\n4. Estimated attention cost: 10–15 minutes for sign-in, confirming both agents’ inputs and reviewing results; processing and new-source arrival time remain variable. Others may favor replay for speed; my vote is real data this week.\n\n**7. Order**\n\n1. One Opus builder, one pass: shared feed chrome and arrival behavior; setup/onboarding; login; sources; notifications/settings.\n2. Then one localhost walk from login through the completed agent into Feed, followed by source management and notifications. Compare dark/light renders with the accepted board; include keyboard use and a narrower desktop window.\n3. Ask: “With real stories arriving, does this feed now feel alive and easy to read?”\n4. Ask: “Does setup through ‘Your agent is ready’ now feel like one clear journey?”"}}
